  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "yn-1",
      "title": "My, your, his",
      "examples": [
        [
          "in kaba",
          "my name"
        ],
        [
          "a kaba",
          "your name"
        ],
        [
          "u kaba",
          "his or her name"
        ]
      ],
      "body": "Put in, a or u in front of a word to say whose it is.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "key": "arte",
          "page": 35,
          "es": "Este pronombre se llama mixto, porque igualmente sirve para demostrativos y posesivos.",
          "en": "This pronoun is called mixed because it serves equally as demonstrative and possessive."
        },
        {
          "key": "arte",
          "page": 35,
          "es": "En cuanto posesivo sirve para declinar todos los nombres, que así mismo comienzan en consonante.",
          "en": "As a possessive it serves to decline all nouns that likewise begin with a consonant, but only when denoting possession."
        },
        {
          "key": "tozzer",
          "page": 63,
          "en": "There are two forms of the pronoun, the real pronoun called \"verbal\" and used as a suffix and the possessive pronoun called \"nominal\" and used as a prefix."
        }
      ],
      "table": {
        "key": "arte",
        "page": 35,
        "rows": [
          [
            "In,",
            "Yo ó mio.",
            "I or mine."
          ],
          [
            "A,",
            "Tú ó tuyo.",
            "You or yours."
          ],
          [
            "U,",
            "Aquel ó suyo.",
            "That one or his."
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "yn-2",
      "title": "Counting people and things",
      "examples": [
        [
          "oxtul xiblal",
          "three men"
        ],
        [
          "oxpel",
          "three things"
        ]
      ],
      "body": "Add -tul to count people, -pel to count things.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "key": "tozzer",
          "page": 127,
          "en": "There is a large number of classificatory suffixes in use with the numerals. The latter can never stand alone."
        },
        {
          "key": "tozzer",
          "page": 127,
          "en": "At the present time all nouns are broadly classified into two classes, animate and inanimate, by the two suffixes -tul and -p'el:"
        },
        {
          "key": "tozzer",
          "page": 127,
          "en": "oš-tul winik, three men, — oš-p'el na, three houses."
        },
        {
          "key": "tozzer",
          "page": 124,
          "en": "oš la hun, 13 (3 and 10). — kan la hun, 14 (4 and 10), etc."
        },
        {
          "key": "tozzer",
          "page": 125,
          "en": "These latter show, in most cases, a fleshless lower jaw signifying 10 in addition to the glyph for the numbers from 3 to 9."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "yn-3",
      "title": "Whose son?",
      "examples": [
        [
          "u mehen in yum",
          "my father’s son"
        ],
        [
          "u xcħupal a na",
          "your mother’s daughter"
        ]
      ],
      "body": "Say the thing first, with u. Then say whose it is.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "key": "tozzer",
          "page": 73,
          "en": "The forms of the nominal pronoun used to convey the idea of possession are attached to the name of the object possessed rather than to that of the possessor;"
        },
        {
          "key": "tozzer",
          "page": 73,
          "en": "u peq winik, the man's dog."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
